   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In August 2020, the Federation of Trade Unions of Uzbekistan (FTUU) submitted a proposal to the National Tripartite Commission on Social and Labour Issues (TCSLI) to address employment and OSH related aspects of seasonal workers in the cotton-picking sector. These workers have often been exposed to irregular recruitment practices amounting at times to forced labour. TCSLI supported the proposal and called on the Government to streamline the highly criticized system of seasonal recruitment of cotton pickers and to modify recruitment rules in a way that would guarantee freedom of association and the right to organize for seasonal workers, negotiate wages and other terms of employment, including measures to introduce minimum hygiene and sanitation conditions. A subsequent Government Resolution (No. 545), issued on 8 September 2020, allowed cotton pickers to freely join trade unions and to negotiate terms of employment. Furthermore, in line with the TCSLI recommendation, the Resolution mandated the Ministry of Health to establish proper medical services for cotton pickers in order to prevent spread of COVID-19 infection in the cotton fields. 
In November 2020, the FTUU Congress adopted its five year Strategy for the period 2020-2025 It stated a clear goal to protect, integrate and represent workers in the informal economy, seasonal workers and those employed in new work arrangements. Based on the new strategy, FTUU adopted recommendations for its affiliates to adjust policies, structures and rules to the needs of workers in the informal economy, including allowing for temporary dual union membership. This has contributed to the recruitment of around 900,000 cotton pickers, roughly 45 per cent of all seasonal cotton pickers across the country. The workers that previously were considered as self-employed in the informal economy, have gained access to trade union representation and services, and better OSH conditions. As part of its new Strategy, FTUU also established a n The ILO provided policy advice to FTUU and its affiliated leaders on the issues of informal economy and irregular self-employment. The advice led to the adjustment of union structures and services and made them more inclusive and effective. 
In October 2020, ILO co-organized a workshop for Uzbekistan trade union leaders on innovative ways of organizing and expanding trade union coverage to workers in the informal economy, self-employed and those in labour platforms. This contributed to the development of the Strategy adopted at the FTUU Congress in November 2020.  
Four regional webinars were delivered by the ILO in April-July 2020 on trade unions&apos; response strategies towards the COVID-19 pandemic:
1. Disrupting Lives, Economies and Societies: Challenges and Prospects for Trade Union Actions;
2. Stimulating economy and employment during Covid19 pandemic;
3. Supporting enterprises, jobs and incomes in times of crises; and
4. Relying on Social Dialogue for Solutions in times of crises.
These webinars informed the FTUU initiatives to the TCSLI and subsequently influenced the Cabinet of the Ministers&apos; decisions regarding fair recruitment and health and safety measures during the 2020 cotton harvest.  
Two ILO knowledge-sharing workshops on integrated strategies, implementing tools and policy approaches for the transition from the informal to the formal economy, held in the framework of the SSTC project, were delivered by the ILO Turin Center in June 2021 for trade unionists from Azerbaijan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan and and Uzbekistan.  These workshops contributed to the FTUU decision to establish a trade union of Mardikors (day labourers). 
The ILO guide on organizing workers in the informal economy was translated into Russian and informed strategy decisions and actions of the FTUU to organize seasonal workers during the 2020 cotton harvest. 
The ILO report Trade Unions in the Balance was translated into Uzbek language and was used to develop FTUU&apos;s strategy on</narrative>
   </description>
